Z||000000||Lou Christopher||12-05-2001||12:03 PM||||I recently picked up a 2.5, 4 speed coupe to drive to save miles on the V8.  In checking it over I filled the trans with 10 -30 oil.  next day I saw oil on the ground.  Put the car on my lift for a day to study leak.  it is coming out from behind the flywheel very slowly.  It is not an axle seal or anything exposed.  I cleaned it off 4 or five times to make sure where the leak source was.  I have never seen the input shaft seal fail on other cars.  Is this common.  My barn is not heated an the prospect of pulling the trans and replacing the seal is not good.  Anyone have any ideas?<BR>                     Lou||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||12-05-2001||12:46 PM||||Leaking "from behind the flywheel" means it's collecting on the trans. side face of the flywheel, I assume?  Sounds like you know what you're doing, and took care track down the source of the leak.  That being the case, if you're quite sure it isn't leaking from the engine...<P>I think all that leaves is, the trans. input seal leaking.  As far as I know, it's not a common problem.  I don't know of a good way to fix it other than R&R the trans.  Temp. fix: Slightly heavier lube, to slow down the leak?<P>...not that you haven't thought about all this already  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR> <BR>||||reg||5
